      "summary": "Verbeke, Rein, Hogan, Loré & Pardi (Immunity 2022) review of innate immune mechanisms of nucleoside-modified mRNA-LNP vaccines (BNT162b2/mRNA-1273). Core mechanism story: (1) Karikó/Weissman 2005 discovery that replacing uridine with pseudouridine derivatives lets IVT mRNA evade TLR3/7/8, RIG-I, MDA5, PKR and OAS sensing while boosting translation — both approved vaccines replace every uridine with N1-methylpseudouridine (m1Ψ); m1Ψ-luciferase mRNA-iLNP expresses ~10-fold higher total protein for 13 days vs 8 days unmodified in mice (Pardi 2018). (2) iLNPs (ionizable lipid pKa ~6.0–6.8 + PEG-lipid + cholesterol + DSPC; SM-102 Moderna, ALC-0315 Pfizer/BioNTech) are neutral at pH 7.4, protonate in the acidifying endosome to disrupt membranes and release mRNA into the cytosol. (3) Paradigm shift: the iLNP carrier itself is the dominant adjuvant — empty LNPs lacking ionizable lipid fail to induce strong antibody responses, iLNP-induced IL-6 is required for optimal Tfh/germinal-center B cell responses in mice; MDA5-IFNα signaling drives the CD8+ T cell response to BNT162b2. GC B/Tfh responses persist up to 6 months post-second dose; spike mRNA/protein detectable in draining-node germinal centers up to 60 days. NOTE: auto-title 'References' came from JATS XML parsing; body is full review text via Europe PMC fullTextXML.",
