      "word_count": 13297,
      "summary": "Schoenmaker et al. 2021 Int J Pharm 601:120586 'mRNA-lipid nanoparticle COVID-19 vaccines: Structure and stability' (PMC mirror; 1286+ citations — canonical early stability review by Crommelin/Jiskoot/Kulkarni groups). Diagnoses WHY mRNA vaccines need cold chains: chemical degradation (hydrolysis) of mRNA dominates over physical instability, initiated by water exposure, modulated by pH; LNP colloidal instability adds aggregation/mRNA-leakage pathways. Documents the deployment-era asymmetry: EMA labels required Moderna at -15 to -25°C vs BioNTech/Pfizer at -60 to -90°C, while both were stable at 2-8°C for only 5 vs 30 days respectively and CureVac's candidate claimed 3 months refrigerated. Raises the unresolved question whether the -70°C requirement reflects true instability or conservative test protocols (Acuitas CEO Madden: the two vaccines 'likely have the same stability'). Improvement levers ranked: optimize mRNA nucleotide composition first, then pH optimization inside/outside LNP, PEG-lipid alternatives, and lyophilization — freeze-dried mRNA with trehalose held stability at 4°C for up to 10 months (Jones et al.). Argues reduced water exposure is the most promising path to refrigerator-temperature shelf life.",
