      "summary": "Sandvik, Kovács & Comes commentary (International Health Policies blog, Nov 25 2020) on the logistics and ethics of deploying Pfizer-BioNTech's ultra-cold-chain vaccine in low- and middle-income countries, written pre-EUA when Pfizer required -70°C storage. Key operational specifics: thermal shippers with active (power cell) + passive (dry ice) cooling hold the required temperature for only 72 hours unopened, forcing air freight; dry ice on passenger aircraft is quantity-regulated because it sublimates and displaces oxygen; vaccine vials remain usable only ~24 hours after a package is opened; proposed pack sizes of 5,000 vs 1,000 doses assume high-throughput sites and effectively exclude low-density rural areas. Ethics argument: ultra-cold requirements plus GAVI AMC co-payments (est. US.60–2/dose for the 92 ODA-eligible countries) create 'chilling effects' — crowding out routine health spending and narrowing pandemic problem-framing to a single vaccine solution. Context: ~200 candidates in pipeline Nov 2020 (48 clinical, 164 preclinical); UNICEF 6-week tender from Nov 12.",
