 A vaccine needs a freezer because its active ingredient is a molecule evolution built to destroy. Both deployed COVID-19 vaccines are manufactured as in vitro transcribed (IVT) mRNA — an RNA strand copied by a bacteriophage enzyme from a DNA template — carrying a 5′ cap ribosomes require, untranslated regions, a codon-optimized gene for SARS-CoV-2 spike locked in its pre-fusion shape by two proline substitutions, and a poly-A tail [[lock]]. Every uridine is replaced by N1-methylpseudouridine (m1Ψ), the modification descending from Karikó and Weissman's 2005 discovery that altered bases slip RNA past innate antiviral sensors [[mrna-vaccines-in-the-covid-19-pandemic-and-beyond-annual-reviews]]; substitution produced "~20 times more total luciferase protein and a longer duration of measurable protein expression" — thirteen days of output that made low-dose vaccines feasible [[acknowledgments]].
 
