-Sequence-level engineering edits the transcript's letters — swapped bases, recoded codons, reshaped folds — and earned everything except shelf life. The heading's other two families need honest books. Cap engineering — enzymatic or co-transcriptional capping — sets yield and innate-sensor evasion, but the corpus ties it to storage nowhere; codon design is subtler: recoding reshapes GC content and secondary structure, the terrain where Section 2's hydrolysis strikes. Without m1Ψ no deployable product exists at any temperature: the twentyfold gain above made tolerable doses and fast pivotal trials possible. Storage is another column. The authoritative pharmaceutical-science review declines the tempting equation: modified nucleosides improve translation and immunogenicity profiles "but do not, by themselves, confer chemical stability against hydrolysis" — m1Ψ swaps a base, not the backbone [[lock-2]]. Linkage-level help is sign-unpredictable: probing found uridine-upstream hotspots that Ψ/m1Ψ mitigates, mO5U worsened thermal stability versus plain uridine, and AUP-minimized recoding bought a twofold stabilization — yet no controlled liquid-formulation m1Ψ-versus-unmodified shelf-life trial exists anywhere indexed across 2005–2026 [[acknowledgement]]. The era's natural experiment agrees: CureVac's CVnCoV claimed three months refrigerated on completely unmodified uridines — though its European rolling review was withdrawn unfinished, leaving a manufacturer claim, not endorsed data [[references-2]] [[lock-3]]. Verdict: decisive viability and timing credit; single digits to at most ten percent direct storage credit.
